<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashikaga Yoshiteru</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1536">1536</a>–<a href="/years/1565">1565</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashikaga Yoshiteru was the thirteenth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a> of the Ashikaga shogunate, holding the office from 1546 to 1565. For much of his rule he was overshadowed or driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</a>, while trying to restore shogunal authority by mediating between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> and studying swordsmanship. In 1565 forces of the Miyoshi elders and Matsunaga Hisamichi attacked his residence and killed him, an event known as the Eiroku Incident.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiteru.jpg" alt="Ashikaga Yoshiteru" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Attributed to Tosa Mitsuyoshi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashikaga_Yoshiteru_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Yoshiteru was born on 31 March 1536, the eldest son of the twelfth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, Ashikaga Yoshiharu, and of a daughter of Konoe Hisamichi; his childhood name was Kikubemaru. When his father was forced to retire in 1546 after a political struggle with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hosokawa-harumoto">Hosokawa Harumoto</a>, Yoshiteru became shogun at the age of 11, invested at Sakamoto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a> outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. He was then called Yoshifushi and took the name Yoshiteru in 1554. His younger brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashikaga-yoshiaki">Ashikaga Yoshiaki</a> later became the fifteenth shogun.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Harumoto's retainer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-nagayoshi">Miyoshi Nagayoshi</a> went over to Hosokawa Ujitsuna, and the war between the two Hosokawa drove Yoshiteru, his father and Harumoto from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>; Yoshiharu died in Ōmi in 1550. Yoshiteru made peace with Nagayoshi in 1552, fought him again with the help of Rokkaku Yoshikata, and was driven out once more in 1558. Nagayoshi, fearing to be accused of killing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogun</a>, took him back under his influence and remained the real power in Kyoto.</p><p>Even so, Yoshiteru tried to make peace between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shimazu-takahisa">Shimazu Takahisa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otomo-sorin">Ōtomo Yoshishige</a>, Mōri Motonari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amago-haruhisa">Amago Haruhisa</a>, and gave the character teru of his name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mori-terumoto">Mōri Terumoto</a>. Oda Nobunaga and Uesugi Kenshin travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a> to pay their respects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Nagayoshi died of illness in 1564, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matsunaga-hisahide">Matsunaga Hisahide</a> and the Miyoshi Triumvirs sought to put Ashikaga Yoshihide in his place. In 1565 Matsunaga Hisamichi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miyoshi-yoshitsugu">Miyoshi Yoshitsugu</a> besieged the buildings where Yoshiteru lived, later the site of Nijō Castle, and no <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> came to his aid in time. The Jesuit Luís Fróis wrote that his squire Odachidono took his own life when his master died. Yoshiteru practised the sword regularly, and Tsukahara Bokuden, founder of the Kashima Shintō-ryū, was among his teachers, so he was called the Kengo shōgun. Many researchers regard him as the last effective holder of the office.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ashikaga Yoshiteru” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1565 the shōgun <b>Ashikaga Yoshiteru</b> was assassinated, and the daimyō Oda Nobunaga used the opening to install Yoshiteru's brother Ashikaga Yoshiaki as shōgun.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ashikaga-shogunate">Ashikaga shogunate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>After Nagayoshi died in 1564, his heir Yoshitsugu, the three Miyoshi elders and Matsunaga Hisahide's son Hisamichi killed the shogun <b>Ashikaga Yoshiteru</b> in 1565 but then fought each other.</blockquote><a href="/state/miyoshi-clan-domain">Miyoshi clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nanzen-ji Temple, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. Died in Kyoto, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.79&amp;year=1554" aria-label="Nanzen-ji Temple on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7992 897 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ashikaga Yoshiteru"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8442 1167h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8442 1167h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.01,135.79&amp;year=1554">What was here in 1554? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
